1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
You wake up at 7:12 and the

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:03,580
world is slow.

3
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:07,580
A glass slides off your nightstand and falls

4
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:08,820
like it's underwater.

5
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:10,800
You don't reach for it.

6
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:12,200
Something else does.

7
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:16,380
A huge pale arm, blue and purple, comes

8
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:18,740
out of your shoulder and puts the glass

9
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:21,680
back on the table before your own hand

10
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:22,680
has even moved.

11
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:27,240
You've woken up with Star Platinum, Jotaro's Stand

12
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:29,100
from JoJo's Bizarre Adventure.

13
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:30,760
And before tonight,

14
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:33,560
it's going to cost you something you can't

15
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:33,880
replace.

16
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:36,460
Nobody else can see it.

17
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:39,140
Your mum walks in and asks why you're

18
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:40,220
staring at the wardrobe.

19
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:43,200
In the story this is called a Stand,

20
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:45,660
and Star Platinum's base powers are simple:

21
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,800
strength, speed and precision, all far beyond a

22
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:50,020
human.

23
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:52,780
You think "left" and it's already moved.

24
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:56,200
Lesson one: you're not the strong one.

25
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,040
Something next to you is.

26
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,740
At noon you go out for bread, because

27
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:03,000
you're trying to act normal.

28
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:06,700
A kid's trolley clips a tower of tinned

29
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:08,920
tomatoes, two metres tall, and it starts to

30
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:09,140
fall.

31
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:13,060
You flinch, and Star Platinum catches all of

32
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:13,420
it.

33
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:16,400
Forty tins, back on the shelf, labels out.

34
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:18,360
Then it keeps going.

35
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:20,360
It straightens the shelf next to it.

36
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:21,980
Then the pasta display.

37
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:25,560
The manager watches forty tins fly back into

38
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:28,720
place all by themselves, and you leave without

39
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:29,280
any bread.

40
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:32,860
Lesson two: it doesn't know when to stop.

41
00:01:33,340 --> 00:01:34,760
You have to tell it.

42
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:37,520
That evening you take it to an empty

43
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:37,780
gym.

44
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:40,800
One of the heaviest deadlifts ever recorded is

45
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,220
about five hundred and ten kilograms, lifted by

46
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:45,720
Hafthor Bjornsson in twenty twenty-five.

47
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:47,080
A human at the very edge.

48
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:49,700
You load every plate in the building onto

49
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:50,300
one bar.

50
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:52,860
Star Platinum picks it up with one hand,

51
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:54,000
like a shopping bag.

52
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:56,140
The bar bends in the middle like a

53
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:56,540
banana.

54
00:01:57,280 --> 00:01:59,480
The owner walks in, looks at the bar,

55
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:02,460
looks at you, and cancels your membership on

56
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:02,880
the spot.

57
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:04,240
Lesson three:

58
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:05,620
strength is easy.

59
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:08,140
Putting things down gently is the hard part.

60
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:11,280
You get home and your hands are shaking,

61
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:12,560
and you notice something:

62
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:15,900
everything it does, you feel afterwards.

63
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:19,220
Every fan knows what comes next.

64
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:21,680
ORA ORA ORA.

65
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:23,160
So you try it on a heavy bag.

66
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:26,300
Real boxers' punches move at roughly six to

67
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:27,560
eleven metres per second.

68
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:30,140
Now say, just as an example, that a

69
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:32,680
Stand throws twenty punches a second at that

70
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:33,000
speed.

71
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:35,660
The bag lasts four seconds.

72
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:36,780
Then it splits,

73
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:39,440
and sand pours across the floor like an

74
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:39,940
hourglass.

75
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:41,780
You sit down in it.

76
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:42,880
You're not hungry.

77
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:44,180
You're starving.

78
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:46,180
You eat three dinners that night.

79
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:48,180
You've started calling it Gary.

80
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:50,100
It doesn't answer to Gary.

81
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:51,300
Lesson four:

82
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
every punch has to be paid for, and

83
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:54,880
you're the one who pays.

84
00:02:56,340 --> 00:02:58,780
At night your keys fall through a drain

85
00:02:58,780 --> 00:02:59,040
grate.

86
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,100
In the story, Star Platinum has a move

87
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:04,980
called Star Finger: it stretches two of its

88
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:07,160
fingers to reach further than its arms can.

89
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:10,660
Two fingers slide down between the bars like

90
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:13,080
chopsticks, and come back up with your keys,

91
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:17,000
a two-euro coin, and somebody's wedding ring.

92
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:18,980
You hand the ring in at the police

93
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:19,400
station.

94
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:22,020
You don't explain how you got it.

95
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:25,520
Lesson five: the useful tricks are the small

96
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:25,880
ones.

97
00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:29,320
A boy in the park films you catching

98
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:31,060
his football before it hits a pram.

99
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:33,820
In the video you're standing still, and the

100
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:36,480
ball just stops in the air, a metre

101
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:37,080
from the baby.

102
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:39,220
Some people say it's edited.

103
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:40,940
Some people say it's a ghost.

104
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:44,520
Nobody says "Stand", because the only people who

105
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:46,120
know what that is think it's a joke.

106
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:47,760
Lesson six:

107
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:50,400
the ones who'd understand are the ones who'd

108
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:51,300
never believe it.

109
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:55,160
Fans love the scenes where Star Platinum catches

110
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:55,520
bullets.

111
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:58,780
So you ask how fast is fast enough.

112
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:00,240
You don't test it.

113
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:01,040
You never would.

114
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:02,640
You do it on a whiteboard.

115
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:06,300
Human visual reaction time is about a quarter

116
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:08,697
of a second, between one hundred and ninety

117
00:04:08,697 --> 00:04:10,020
and two hundred and eighty-five milliseconds.

118
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:13,440
A nine-millimetre pistol bullet leaves the barrel at

119
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:15,740
around three hundred and fifty to four hundred

120
00:04:15,740 --> 00:04:17,260
and ten metres per second.

121
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:20,320
Our own maths: at three hundred and sixty,

122
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:23,280
it crosses ten metres in about twenty-eight milliseconds.

123
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:26,560
That's about nine times shorter than your reaction

124
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:26,980
time.

125
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:30,480
Usain Bolt, the fastest human ever measured,

126
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:32,740
averaged about ten metres per second.

127
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:34,200
You put the marker down.

128
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,860
There's one thing in the story that doesn't

129
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:38,100
depend on speed at all.

130
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:40,700
Near the end of Part three, Jotaro learns

131
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:41,600
to stop time,

132
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:43,500
about five seconds at first.

133
00:04:44,180 --> 00:04:44,840
You don't have that.

134
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:46,020
You think.

135
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:50,140
Lesson seven: being fast doesn't make you fast

136
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:50,960
enough for everything.

137
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:55,000
By day thirty you can't stop catching things.

138
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:55,680
Cups.

139
00:04:56,180 --> 00:04:56,520
Phones.

140
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:57,880
A tray of glasses.

141
00:04:58,280 --> 00:04:59,640
A toddler falling off a wall.

142
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:02,040
Your shoulders are always tight.

143
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:04,260
You watch the edges of every table.

144
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:06,780
Your friends notice you never relax.

145
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:09,080
Your hands shake every evening.

146
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:12,020
Lesson eight: when you can catch everything,

147
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:14,820
you start feeling responsible for everything.

148
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:18,760
On day forty, a delivery van rolls down

149
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:20,400
a hill with nobody in it.

150
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:23,180
At the bottom there's a school crossing, and

151
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:25,240
the lollipop lady has just stepped out.

152
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:26,460
You run.

153
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:28,120
You're not going to make it.

154
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:30,140
Star Platinum isn't going to make it either.

155
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,240
So you do the only thing you've never

156
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:32,580
tried.

157
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:34,220
You hold your breath.

158
00:05:34,900 --> 00:05:35,720
The world goes silent.

159
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:37,940
The van hangs in the air like a

160
00:05:37,940 --> 00:05:38,320
photograph.

161
00:05:38,980 --> 00:05:41,160
The birds stop mid-wing.

162
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:41,960
You reach the van.

163
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:44,660
Star Platinum puts both hands on the bonnet

164
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:46,000
and turns it toward a hedge.

165
00:05:46,740 --> 00:05:47,500
You step back.

166
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:48,900
Your lungs burn.

167
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:50,440
That's all you get:

168
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:51,680
one breath.

169
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:53,540
And the world starts again.

170
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:56,040
The van hits the hedge at walking speed.

171
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:57,460
Nobody is hurt.

172
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:01,040
The lollipop lady blinks at you, and keeps

173
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:01,780
holding her sign.

174
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:03,760
The next morning

175
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:05,440
you can't lift your hands,

176
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:08,040
and they don't stop shaking until lunch.

177
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:10,940
Now you know what it really is.

178
00:06:11,820 --> 00:06:12,660
Not a superpower.

179
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,880
A second body that never gets tired,

180
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:17,640
attached to one that does.

181
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,240
Lesson nine: the strongest thing about you is

182
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:24,620
still the most tired thing about you.

183
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,020
The next morning, 7:12.

184
00:06:28,020 --> 00:06:30,000
A glass slides off your nightstand.

185
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,040
This time you reach first.

186
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:33,080
You miss.

187
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:36,360
And something catches it anyway.

188
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:39,080
So if you woke up tomorrow with Star

189
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:39,520
Platinum,

190
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:42,100
would you keep it, or give it back?

191
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:44,780
And what's the first thing you'd catch?

192
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:46,140
Tell me in the comments.
